=== Step 1: checking your PC ===
  [ok] GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 Ti, 15.9 GB VRAM, compute capability 12.0, driver 595.84
  [ok] RAM: 504 GB
  [ok] CPU: Intel(R) Xeon(R) CPU E5-2695 v4 @ 2.10GHz (AVX2)
  [ok] PCIe: 3.0 x8 (the card supports 5.0, the board 3.0) - up to ~8 GB/s to the GPU: long prompts are read slower than on a PCIe 4.0 x16 PC (their experts are copied over it); the engine measures the link at start for the output speed
  [!]  the GPU's PCIe link reads 8 of its 16 lanes right now (some cards narrow it when idle); if it stays narrow under load: is it in the right slot (the one wired x16), fully seated, and not sharing lanes with an M.2 drive? (the BIOS can say)

=== Step 2: your choices ===
  [ok] model: Qwen3.8-Flash-Next

  1) Q2_0     2-bit, the fastest; download 66 GB, uses ~34 GB of RAM
  2) IQ2_XS   2-bit i-quant, a little better quality, close in speed; download 68 GB, uses ~36 GB of RAM
  3) IQ3_XXS  3-bit i-quant, better quality, slower (more CPU work per token); download 76 GB, uses ~43 GB of RAM
  4) IQ3_S    (recommended) 3.5-bit i-quant, the best quality (matches the full model), the slowest; needs a 64 GB PC with little else running; download 84 GB, uses ~50 GB of RAM
  [ok] size: IQ3_S

  Context length = how much text the model can see at once (your chat, files, tool output).
  Longer needs more VRAM for it, so fewer experts fit on the GPU:
  1) 8K tokens
  2) 32K tokens
  3) 64K tokens   (recommended for your GPU)
  4) 128K tokens
  5) 200K tokens
  6) 256K tokens
  7) 384K tokens   (experimental: setup adds rope scaling)
  8) 512K tokens   (experimental: setup adds rope scaling)
  [ok] context: 65536 tokens
  [ok] KV cache: 8-bit

  Images: the model can also read pictures (screenshots, photos, scanned pages). This adds a 0.9 GB
  download. On a card under 24 GB the encoder runs on the CPU (a few seconds per picture, nothing taken
  from the GPU, pictures read at up to 300 tokens; --vision-tokens 1024 for more detail): the GPU
  encoder keeps ~1.4 GB of VRAM free, which costs 3-5% of text speed on 16 GB cards (measured) and
  more on some PCs. --vision gpu if you want pictures in under a second.
  [ok] images: on (encoder on the CPU)

  EXPERIMENTAL - speed projection: a small control vector applied while the model runs (layers 4-44).
  It changes how the model answers: its package describes it as a refusal-direction projection (the
  model declines far fewer requests). Off unless you choose it; when on, the web app can switch it off
  per chat. Details: data/experimental-speed-projection/README.md
  [ok] experimental speed projection: off

